---
description: Apply when teaching a concept, explaining how or why something works, or writing tutorials and docs
globs:
alwaysApply: false
---

- An explanation is an edit to a model already in the learner's head — locate it first: what they know (the anchor), what they think they know (misconceptions need displacement, not filling), what they need it for (sets depth). Ban "obviously/simply/just"; what follows them is where readers fall off.
- Lead with what the thing is for, one sentence, before any mechanism. Anchor to something they own, and flag where the analogy breaks — unbounded analogies become next year's misconceptions.
- Concrete before abstract, always: a worked example with real values, then the general rule; two contrasting examples (one that is, one that almost-is) draw the boundary better than definitions. One dependency-ordered step at a time.
- Displace misconceptions by name: show where their current model predicts wrongly, then install the replacement — new information poured onto an intact misconception just gets reinterpreted to fit it.
- Simplify honestly: flagged omissions are fine ("ignoring caching for now"); simplifications the learner must later unlearn are not. Park edge cases until the core model stands — early caveats erode it.
- Verify by making them generate: restate in their words, predict an outcome, apply one step beyond the examples. "Does that make sense?" measures politeness. In writing, pose the question, pause, answer. If they can't do anything new afterward, it didn't land.
